# Claude Code — per-user, not shared (the shared .claude/settings.json does stay in the repo)
.claude/settings.local.json

# Local secrets -- this repo is public; never let a token/key land in a tracked file.
.env

# PowerShell's own machine-local caches, which land here when a run resolves its per-user data
# directory to the repo root instead of %LOCALAPPDATA%. Microsoft/Windows/PowerShell/ModuleAnalysisCache
# was committed by accident in 65902dd; PowerShell REWRITES it in place on almost every run, so a tracked
# copy dirties the working tree continuously -- and cut-release.ps1 refuses to run on a dirty tree, which
# blocked the v4.6.0 cut TWICE (restored by hand both times). Untracked on August 13, 2026; the file
# itself stays on disk, since it is PowerShell's to write.
#
# The whole /Microsoft/ tree is ignored rather than that one path, because the cause is the redirected
# directory rather than the file: PowerShell writes startup-profile data as a sibling under the same
# root. Anchored with a leading slash, so this can never silence a legitimately-named folder deeper in
# the tree.
/Microsoft/

# The hosted release-notes page (build-release-notes-page.ps1). Two different reasons, and only the
# first is the ordinary one.
#
# The page, the worker bundle and wrangler's scratch directory are DERIVATIVES of documents that are
# already tracked under releases/audience/ -- rebuildable in a second, and a 200 KB file that changes
# on every release otherwise dirties the tree that cut-release.ps1 refuses to run on.
#
# THE TOKEN IS THE OTHER REASON, and it is about this repo being PUBLIC. The worker serves the page at
# /notes/<32 hex> and that path is the only lock on it, so committing the token here would publish the
# key beside the door. The consequence is deliberate and worth stating: nothing in git remembers the
# URL, so whoever creates it records it outside the repo -- the file itself is the only copy on this
# machine. A consumer whose repository is PRIVATE should commit theirs instead, where a tracked token
# is what survives a lost machine.
#
# AND NOTHING MOVES IT FOR YOU. This pattern names a path, and an ignored file is invisible to `git mv`
# by construction -- so renaming the folder above it leaves the token behind in a directory nothing
# points at any more, which is exactly what became of contributing-davekjohn/releases/page/ on
# September 5, 2026 (#1444). build-release-notes-page.ps1 searches the tree before it refuses or mints
# one, so an orphan is named instead of silently replaced; moving the folder is still yours to do.
dkj-policy/releases/page/

# THE HARNESS WRITES THIS, NOT US (issue #1673). A dispatched agent's own worktree lands INSIDE the
# repo at .claude/worktrees/agent-<id>, which is the harness's choice rather than this repo's -- there is
# no seam here to move it outside the tree the way worktree-lane.ps1 places a lane in a sibling
# '<repo>-lanes/' directory instead.
#
# ANCHORED, for the same reason /Microsoft/ is: so this can never silence a legitimately-named folder
# deeper in the tree.
#
# THE HONEST COST: ignoring it means a worktree that did NOT get cleaned up is now invisible to
# `git status`, which used to be the only thing that noticed one standing. `git worktree list` still
# reports it regardless of this entry -- it reads git's own registry, not the working tree -- which is
# why check-plugin-integrity.ps1 reads THAT rather than relying on a dirty tree to surface it. Also
# note: `git worktree remove` leaves the empty `.claude/worktrees/` directory behind (measured), so an
# empty directory here is not itself evidence that a worktree is still registered.
/.claude/worktrees/

# WRANGLER'S OWN ACCOUNT CACHE, which `npx wrangler` writes into the directory it runs from (#2581). It
# holds the Cloudflare account id and the account name, and that name is an e-mail address. This repo is
# PUBLIC, so the cache is personal information and never belongs in the tree. bbcbdfd7 committed it by
# accident from a run at the repo root, and the rule above only covers the page's own scratch directory.
#
# ANCHORED, like /Microsoft/: the cause is a run at the root, and the rule must not silence a
# legitimately-named folder deeper in the tree.
/.wrangler/
